  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Wednesday Morning: Periodic Rain and Storms\n\nThe line of showers and storms along a cold front that moved \nout of central MN early this morning faded by 9am as the \nattendant upper wave lifted into SW Ontario. The upcoming rain \nfocus shifts to mainly south of I-90 for this afternoon and \ntonight as the lower tropospheric baroclinic zone/theta-e axis \nsags into Iowa and far southern Wisconsin (with convergence \nstrongest in Iowa) and the right-rear quadrant of the upper \nlevel jet arrives. \n\nUpstream radar trends over Nebraska and western Iowa continue \nto support that the highest rainfall amounts will be south of \nthe forecast area, with lighter stratiform precipitation \npossibly advecting as far north as I-90 as we go through the \nafternoon and overnight. Latest HRRR runs support observational \ntrends and take much of the heavier precipitation closer to I-80\nlate tonight. Could still pick up a quick 1/2 to 1 inch of rain\nin pockets along and south of Highway 18 before Wednesday \nmorning given the ample deep moisture present, but otherwise the\nimpacts from this rain look to be minimal.\n\nWednesday Afternoon - Thursday: Dry and Seasonal\n\nCyclonic flow lingers for the remainder of the week with a \nbroad cutoff low centered over the far north-central CONUS and \nsouth- central Canada. Shortwave ridging passes through from \nWednesday afternoon through Thursday with the main surface \nbaroclinic zone displaced to the south, keeping the risk for any\nprecipitation at bay. With the flow remaining weak, minimal \nlower tropospheric temperature advection is expected during this\ntimeframe and highs will only rise into the lower 80s by \nThursday despite the clearer skies.\n\nFriday - Early Next Week: Seasonal Temps, Periodic Storms\n\nGiven the weak flow pattern we find ourselves in with the main \njet stream shifted north to the U.S./Canadian border, confidence\nin the timing and coverage of storms for Friday and the weekend\nis low at this time. The deterministic solutions depict the \npossibility of multiple weak perturbations rotating through \nduring this period, but the guidance differs on how far north \nthe richer Central Plains airmass can advance, which will play \nheavily into the probability of any storms (and surface \ntemperatures). Moving into early next week, the jet stream looks\nto sag southward and provide more support for organized \nconvection. Again, the question of how exactly the thermodynamic\nand kinematic spaces line up will play heavily into storm \nstrength and coverage.",
